WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:04.840
Good morning, John. Yesterday, I let everybody know that I was stuck in, as you say, Cheyenne, Wyoming.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.740
I'm working on my book. Since I've been stuck in book land,

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:10.319
I haven't been thinking about what I'm gonna do on Vlogbrothers, so I asked everybody what I should talk about.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.360
And CGP Grey asked how in the world the word Cheyenne,

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.480
this word, is pronounced

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:21.140
like a girl named Anne who doesn't talk very much? Because his brain sees that word and just says, "Nope,

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.380
that is not a word that can be pronounced."

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:27.640
And I can understand that. And it's probably for an actual reason. Because the word Cheyenne went through several

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:31.940
rapid rounds of what linguists call loanword adaptation.

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:34.700
That's when speakers of one language take a word from another language

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.620
And then they modify the sounds in it so that they can say it.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:42.460
Like, they make it pronounceable with the noises that their language has. In English, the loanword adaptation of

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:45.540
/bʊrˈitɔ/ is /bəɹiˈɾəʊ/, because we don't roll our Rs.

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:50.339
Which something that every language does. So, when French settlers were first learning about people in the western United States,

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.430
they asked the people that they had been talking with, who were Dakota,

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.400
about these other people, who were not Dakota

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.580
and the Dakota gave the French people their word for those people.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:04.720
The French people then adapted that loanword into something that sounded kind of like "shey-en,"

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:10.780
and then, somewhat rapidly, English adapted that loanword from French from "shey-en" to "shy-an,"

00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:15.419
which sounds much more American, right? And I say American because this isn't just about languages, it's about dialects,

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.780
it's about the sounds that we make. Like, "shy-an"

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:23.240
is not the kind of noise that a British person would make. I'm sure they would find a much more lovely way of saying that word.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.040
Loanword adaptation isn't a sign of disrespect for a language,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:32.160
it's a sign that we need this word and we can't say it with the noises that you make because we don't have those noises.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.880
Anyway, this video is about stress and how to deal with it.

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:37.229
I know that it doesn't seem like it.

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:40.618
Here's the situation: when we experience, like, existential threats,

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:43.940
threats to, like, our being, our identity, our existence,

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:46.720
our stress response is built for physical threats.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:49.500
And so our bodies do a bunch of stuff to prepare us for a physical threat.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.640
They, like, our blood pressure goes up, our heart rate goes up,

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:54.460
resources get diverted from our immune system and from our digestion,

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.440
and we want to do one of two things: We want to fight, or we want to flee.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.120
And the first two minutes of this video, that was me fleeing.

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:06.500
That is an excellent example of a bad, but not worst, strategy.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.320
Because at least now I know stuff I didn't know before.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.760
If I'm thinking about this, like, there's fight and there's flight.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:14.520
And flight is reading about linguistics, or going on Twitter or Facebook or whatever.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.600
So what is fight, right? Like, I can't punch my email inbox,

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:19.660
though certainly I would like to sometimes. A week ago,

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:23.580
I got a bunch of bad news in a row. And I was sitting right here at this desk

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:28.040
and I remember thinking, like, that I had spent like a half an hour just

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:32.360
trying to distract myself and running away from this unpleasantness.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.500
It was like a bunch of bad things, unrelated to each other,

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.209
so it was just, like, piling on top of me, right?

00:02:36.209 --> 00:02:41.640
And it didn't get better until I interfaced with it. And that might be thinking about it, or writing an email,

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:47.840
or talking to somebody about it; but dealing with it, letting it be in my life, that's the fight.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:53.969
It's remarkable, kind of, but thinking about the unpleasantness, makes the unpleasantness so much less unpleasant.

00:02:53.969 --> 00:02:59.660
But because it is unpleasant, you don't want to do it at all. And then I sent a bunch of emails, and the, the fight…

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:04.760
was over, and I had dealt with it, and I was through it, and I wasn't stressed out anymore.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:07.780
And I could play with my baby and be happy. I know this isn't always possible;

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:12.820
my worst stresses in my life have been things that I can't fix, that you can't interface with. [High piano note coincides with arrow appearing]

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:16.040
They're just things that happened, because bad things happen.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:18.140
That, you just accept, and that's no fun.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:22.340
But the other things, though, the things that I can, at least, affect in some way.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:26.660
And it can be hard to find the difference between those two things. I found that kind of nothing works except,

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:29.700
like, overpowering my lizard brain and

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:35.180
actually doing the things that I know will help. Whether that's exercise, or contemplation,

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:39.780
or talking to somebody about it. Not running away to Reddit, not learning about linguistics,

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.800
interfacing with the thing that is causing the stress.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:49.460
And fighting, you know? In, like, the most unnatural of ways. Like, slowly, and methodically,

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:54.580
and with the knowledge that nothing is going to fix this except work.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:56.580
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

